{
 "version": 1,
 "id": "dbdf32c1-18cb-46cc-b830-7fce88591072",
 "title": "Sans-IO: a network handshake with the network removed",
 "created_at": "2026-07-10T00:00:00Z",
 "updated_at": "2026-07-12T00:00:00Z",
 "shared_cargo_toml": "[profile.release]\nopt-level = 1\nlto = false\ncodegen-units = 16",
 "shared_source": "// \u2500\u2500 The protocol: a sans-IO STUN-style binding handshake \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n//\n// Zero dependencies. No sockets. No clock. No `Instant::now()` anywhere below.\n// You feed the machine packets and timestamps; it tells you what to send and\n// when to wake it. These same bytes run under tokio, in a no_std firmware loop,\n// and in the browser wasm sandbox that is rendering this notebook.\n\n/// Milliseconds on some clock the caller owns. Always injected, never sampled.\npub type Millis = u64;\n\n/// A datagram, as our toy protocol sees it.\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]\npub enum Packet {\n    /// Binding request, carrying a transaction id.\n    Request { txid: u32 },\n    /// Binding success, echoing the txid and the port the server saw.\n    Response { txid: u32, mapped_port: u16 },\n}\n\n/// Where the handshake is right now.\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]\npub enum State {\n    /// Constructed; nothing sent yet.\n    Idle,\n    /// In flight. `attempt` is 1-based; `deadline` is when to retransmit.\n    Waiting { attempt: u32, deadline: Millis },\n    /// The server answered; we learned our reflexive port.\n    Bound { mapped_port: u16 },\n    /// Retransmit budget exhausted.\n    Failed,\n}\n\n/// The entire handshake: four methods, one `match`, a `Vec` for the outbox.\npub struct StunBinding {\n    txid: u32,\n    state: State,\n    outbox: Vec<Packet>,\n    rto: Millis,\n    max_attempts: u32,\n}\n\nimpl StunBinding {\n    /// Begin a transaction. Queues the first request immediately.\n    pub fn new(txid: u32, now: Millis, rto: Millis, max_attempts: u32) -> Self {\n        let mut binding = Self {\n            txid,\n            state: State::Idle,\n            outbox: Vec::new(),\n            rto,\n            max_attempts,\n        };\n        binding.arm(now, 1);\n        binding\n    }\n\n    /// Queue a request and set the next retransmit deadline.\n    fn arm(&mut self, now: Millis, attempt: u32) {\n        self.outbox.push(Packet::Request { txid: self.txid });\n        // RFC 5389 \u00a77.2.1: the retransmit timeout doubles on each attempt.\n        let backoff = self.rto.saturating_mul(1u64 << (attempt - 1).min(6));\n        self.state = State::Waiting {\n            attempt,\n            deadline: now.saturating_add(backoff),\n        };\n    }\n\n    /// Feed in a received datagram.\n    pub fn handle_input(&mut self, packet: Packet, _now: Millis) {\n        if let (\n            State::Waiting { .. },\n            Packet::Response {\n                txid,\n                mapped_port,\n            },\n        ) = (self.state, packet)\n        {\n            if txid == self.txid {\n                self.state = State::Bound { mapped_port };\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// Advance timers. On a missed deadline, retransmit or give up.\n    pub fn handle_timeout(&mut self, now: Millis) {\n        if let State::Waiting { attempt, deadline } = self.state {\n            if now >= deadline {\n                if attempt >= self.max_attempts {\n                    self.state = State::Failed;\n                } else {\n                    self.arm(now, attempt + 1);\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// Pull the next datagram the machine wants to put on the wire.\n    pub fn poll_transmit(&mut self) -> Option<Packet> {\n        if self.outbox.is_empty() {\n            None\n        } else {\n            Some(self.outbox.remove(0))\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// When should the caller next call `handle_timeout`? `None` means \"never\".\n    pub fn poll_timeout(&self) -> Option<Millis> {\n        match self.state {\n            State::Waiting { deadline, .. } => Some(deadline),\n            _ => None,\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// The current state, for rendering and tests.\n    pub fn state(&self) -> State {\n        self.state\n    }\n}\n\n// \u2500\u2500 A deterministic toy network (still zero I/O) \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\n/// A tiny linear-congruential PRNG, so packet loss is reproducible without a\n/// `rand` dependency. Same seed, same coin flips, every single time.\npub struct Lcg(pub u64);\n\nimpl Lcg {\n    /// Advance the state and return the high bits.\n    pub fn next_u32(&mut self) -> u32 {\n        self.0 = self\n            .0\n            .wrapping_mul(6_364_136_223_846_793_005)\n            .wrapping_add(1_442_695_040_888_963_407);\n        (self.0 >> 33) as u32\n    }\n\n    /// Returns `true` with probability `loss_percent` / 100.\n    pub fn drops(&mut self, loss_percent: u32) -> bool {\n        self.next_u32() % 100 < loss_percent\n    }\n}\n\n// \u2500\u2500 Driving the machine against that network \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\n/// What happened on one step of a driven run, captured for the log table.\n#[derive(Clone)]\npub struct LogRow {\n    pub clock: Millis,\n    pub kind: EventKind,\n    pub detail: String,\n    pub state: String,\n}\n\n/// The flavor of a log row, used only to color its badge.\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]\npub enum EventKind {\n    Send,\n    Recv,\n    Drop,\n    Timer,\n    Fail,\n}\n\n/// A short, readable name for a state (the attempt count folded in).\npub fn state_label(s: State) -> String {\n    match s {\n        State::Idle => \"Idle\".to_string(),\n        State::Waiting { attempt, .. } => format!(\"Waiting (try {attempt})\"),\n        State::Bound { mapped_port } => format!(\"Bound :{mapped_port}\"),\n        State::Failed => \"Failed\".to_string(),\n    }\n}\n\n/// Drive a full handshake against a synthetic lossy network and return the\n/// final state plus a step-by-step log. Pure: same arguments, same result,\n/// forever. There is no socket and no wall clock anywhere in here.\npub fn run(\n    txid: u32,\n    seed: u64,\n    rto: Millis,\n    max_attempts: u32,\n    loss_percent: u32,\n) -> (State, Vec<LogRow>) {\n    let mut binding = StunBinding::new(txid, 0, rto, max_attempts);\n    let mut net = Lcg(seed);\n    let mut log: Vec<LogRow> = Vec::new();\n    let mut clock: Millis = 0;\n\n    // A safety cap so a pathological config can never spin forever.\n    for _ in 0..64 {\n        // 1. Send everything the machine has queued.\n        let mut response_arrives = false;\n        while let Some(packet) = binding.poll_transmit() {\n            if let Packet::Request { txid } = packet {\n                if net.drops(loss_percent) {\n                    log.push(LogRow {\n                        clock,\n                        kind: EventKind::Drop,\n                        detail: format!(\"request {txid:#010x} lost on the way out\"),\n                        state: state_label(binding.state()),\n                    });\n                } else if net.drops(loss_percent) {\n                    log.push(LogRow {\n                        clock,\n                        kind: EventKind::Drop,\n                        detail: format!(\"request {txid:#010x} arrives; the reply is lost\"),\n                        state: state_label(binding.state()),\n                    });\n                } else {\n                    log.push(LogRow {\n                        clock,\n                        kind: EventKind::Send,\n                        detail: format!(\"request {txid:#010x} reaches the server\"),\n                        state: state_label(binding.state()),\n                    });\n                    response_arrives = true;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n\n        // 2. If a reply survived, hand it back to the machine.\n        if response_arrives {\n            binding.handle_input(\n                Packet::Response {\n                    txid,\n                    mapped_port: 51820,\n                },\n                clock,\n            );\n            log.push(LogRow {\n                clock,\n                kind: EventKind::Recv,\n                detail: \"response accepted; mapped port :51820 learned\".to_string(),\n                state: state_label(binding.state()),\n            });\n        }\n\n        // 3. Are we finished?\n        if matches!(binding.state(), State::Bound { .. } | State::Failed) {\n            break;\n        }\n\n        // 4. Nothing came back: jump the clock to the deadline and tick timers.\n        let Some(deadline) = binding.poll_timeout() else {\n            break;\n        };\n        clock = deadline;\n        binding.handle_timeout(clock);\n        match binding.state() {\n            State::Failed => log.push(LogRow {\n                clock,\n                kind: EventKind::Fail,\n                detail: format!(\"silence after {max_attempts} attempts; giving up\"),\n                state: state_label(binding.state()),\n            }),\n            State::Waiting { attempt, .. } => log.push(LogRow {\n                clock,\n                kind: EventKind::Timer,\n                detail: format!(\"deadline reached; retransmitting (attempt {attempt})\"),\n                state: state_label(binding.state()),\n            }),\n            _ => {}\n        }\n    }\n\n    (binding.state(), log)\n}\n\n// \u2500\u2500 Notebook rendering (glue only; NOT part of the protocol) \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n//\n// Everything below exists purely to draw the machine for this notebook, using\n// nothing but `String` building. The protocol above never mentions SVG, HTML,\n// or the browser: that is exactly the point.\n\n/// Map a state to `(active_index, accent_color)` for the diagram.\nfn active_node(s: State) -> (usize, &'static str) {\n    match s {\n        State::Idle => (0, \"#e94560\"),\n        State::Waiting { .. } => (1, \"#e94560\"),\n        State::Bound { .. } => (2, \"#3ecf8e\"),\n        State::Failed => (3, \"#ff6b6b\"),\n    }\n}\n\n/// Draw the four-state diagram, lighting up whichever state is active.\npub fn render_diagram(active: State) -> String {\n    let (active_idx, accent) = active_node(active);\n\n    // (index, x, y, w, h, label)\n    let nodes = [\n        (0usize, 40, 95, 120, 52, \"Idle\"),\n        (1, 260, 95, 150, 52, \"Waiting\"),\n        (2, 540, 30, 120, 52, \"Bound\"),\n        (3, 540, 160, 120, 52, \"Failed\"),\n    ];\n\n    let mut svg = String::new();\n    svg.push_str(\n        \"<svg viewBox=\\\"0 0 700 250\\\" xmlns=\\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\\" \\\n         style=\\\"width:100%;max-width:700px;background:#161625;border-radius:10px;\\\n         font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,Consolas,monospace\\\">\",\n    );\n    // Arrowhead marker.\n    svg.push_str(\n        \"<defs><marker id=\\\"ah\\\" viewBox=\\\"0 0 10 10\\\" refX=\\\"9\\\" refY=\\\"5\\\" \\\n         markerWidth=\\\"7\\\" markerHeight=\\\"7\\\" orient=\\\"auto-start-reverse\\\">\\\n         <path d=\\\"M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z\\\" fill=\\\"#6b6b8a\\\"/></marker></defs>\",\n    );\n\n    // \u2500\u2500 Edges \u2500\u2500\n    let edge = |from: &str, to: &str, label: &str, lx: i32, ly: i32| -> String {\n        format!(\n            \"<path d=\\\"M{from} L{to}\\\" stroke=\\\"#4a4a66\\\" stroke-width=\\\"2\\\" fill=\\\"none\\\" \\\n             marker-end=\\\"url(#ah)\\\"/>\\\n             <text x=\\\"{lx}\\\" y=\\\"{ly}\\\" fill=\\\"#8888aa\\\" font-size=\\\"12\\\" \\\n             text-anchor=\\\"middle\\\">{label}</text>\"\n        )\n    };\n    // Idle -> Waiting\n    svg.push_str(&edge(\"160,121\", \"252,121\", \"first request\", 210, 112));\n    // Waiting -> Bound\n    svg.push_str(&edge(\"412,108\", \"534,66\", \"response\", 500, 96));\n    // Waiting -> Failed\n    svg.push_str(&edge(\"412,134\", \"534,182\", \"max retries\", 500, 168));\n    // Waiting self-loop (retransmit)\n    svg.push_str(\n        \"<path d=\\\"M300,95 C300,45 370,45 370,95\\\" stroke=\\\"#4a4a66\\\" stroke-width=\\\"2\\\" \\\n         fill=\\\"none\\\" marker-end=\\\"url(#ah)\\\"/>\\\n         <text x=\\\"335\\\" y=\\\"38\\\" fill=\\\"#8888aa\\\" font-size=\\\"12\\\" text-anchor=\\\"middle\\\">\\\n         timeout / retransmit</text>\",\n    );\n\n    // \u2500\u2500 Nodes \u2500\u2500\n    for (idx, x, y, w, h, label) in nodes {\n        let is_active = idx == active_idx;\n        let (fill, stroke, text_color, weight) = if is_active {\n            (accent, accent, \"#ffffff\", \"700\")\n        } else {\n            (\"#232338\", \"#3a3a55\", \"#9a9ab5\", \"500\")\n        };\n        let glow = if is_active {\n            format!(\" filter=\\\"drop-shadow(0 0 6px {accent})\\\"\")\n        } else {\n            String::new()\n        };\n        svg.push_str(&format!(\n            \"<g{glow}><rect x=\\\"{x}\\\" y=\\\"{y}\\\" width=\\\"{w}\\\" height=\\\"{h}\\\" rx=\\\"9\\\" \\\n             fill=\\\"{fill}\\\" stroke=\\\"{stroke}\\\" stroke-width=\\\"2\\\"/>\\\n             <text x=\\\"{tx}\\\" y=\\\"{ty}\\\" fill=\\\"{text_color}\\\" font-size=\\\"16\\\" \\\n             font-weight=\\\"{weight}\\\" text-anchor=\\\"middle\\\">{label}</text></g>\",\n            tx = x + w / 2,\n            ty = y + h / 2 + 6,\n        ));\n    }\n\n    svg.push_str(&format!(\n        \"<text x=\\\"350\\\" y=\\\"242\\\" fill=\\\"#c7c7de\\\" font-size=\\\"13\\\" text-anchor=\\\"middle\\\">\\\n         current state: {}</text>\",\n        state_label(active)\n    ));\n    svg.push_str(\"</svg>\");\n    svg\n}\n\n/// Render a driven run's log as a styled HTML table with color-coded badges.\npub fn render_log(log: &[LogRow]) -> String {\n    let badge = |kind: EventKind| -> (&'static str, &'static str) {\n        match kind {\n            EventKind::Send => (\"SEND\", \"#4fc3f7\"),\n            EventKind::Recv => (\"RECV\", \"#3ecf8e\"),\n            EventKind::Drop => (\"DROP\", \"#ff6b6b\"),\n            EventKind::Timer => (\"TIMER\", \"#ffd166\"),\n            EventKind::Fail => (\"FAIL\", \"#ff6b6b\"),\n        }\n    };\n\n    let mut html = String::new();\n    html.push_str(\n        \"<table style=\\\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;\\\n         font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;font-size:13px;\\\n         background:#161625;color:#e0e0ea;border-radius:8px;overflow:hidden\\\">\",\n    );\n    html.push_str(\n        \"<thead><tr style=\\\"background:#20203a;color:#9a9ab5;text-align:left\\\">\\\n         <th style=\\\"padding:8px 12px\\\">#</th>\\\n         <th style=\\\"padding:8px 12px\\\">clock</th>\\\n         <th style=\\\"padding:8px 12px\\\">event</th>\\\n         <th style=\\\"padding:8px 12px\\\">what happened</th>\\\n         <th style=\\\"padding:8px 12px\\\">state after</th></tr></thead><tbody>\",\n    );\n\n    for (i, row) in log.iter().enumerate() {\n        let (tag, color) = badge(row.kind);\n        html.push_str(&format!(\n            \"<tr style=\\\"border-top:1px solid #2a2a44\\\">\\\n             <td style=\\\"padding:8px 12px;color:#6b6b8a\\\">{n}</td>\\\n             <td style=\\\"padding:8px 12px;color:#c7c7de\\\">{clock} ms</td>\\\n             <td style=\\\"padding:8px 12px\\\">\\\n             <span style=\\\"background:{color}22;color:{color};padding:2px 8px;\\\n             border-radius:4px;font-weight:700\\\">{tag}</span></td>\\\n             <td style=\\\"padding:8px 12px\\\">{detail}</td>\\\n             <td style=\\\"padding:8px 12px;color:#bb9cf0\\\">{state}</td></tr>\",\n            n = i + 1,\n            clock = row.clock,\n            detail = row.detail,\n            state = row.state,\n        ));\n    }\n\n    html.push_str(\"</tbody></table>\");\n    html\n}\n",
 "description": "A STUN-style handshake built as a pure state machine, then run, lost, retransmitted, and replayed deterministically inside a browser wasm cell.",
 "tags": [
  "sans-io",
  "protocol",
  "state-machine",
  "networking",
  "async",
  "determinism",
  "blog"
 ],
 "cells": [
  {
   "id": "cell_0",
   "order": 0,
   "label": "The trick every Rust networking crate reinvents",
   "cell_type": "Markdown",
   "source": "> *AI generated.*\n\n## Introduction\n\nLook at the Rust crates that got protocol design *right* and you find the same move underneath all of them: the protocol logic is there, and the I/O has been surgically removed. No sockets. No timers. No `Instant::now()`. Just a state machine you feed bytes and timestamps.\n\nThey call it **sans-IO**, and the family keeps growing. [quinn-proto](https://docs.rs/quinn-proto) is the QUIC state machine with the networking sliced out. [str0m](https://github.com/algesten/str0m) is a fully synchronous WebRTC stack. And as of **January 2026**, [sctp-proto](https://github.com/algesten/sctp-proto) moved under the str0m maintainers, quietly consolidating a whole sans-IO protocol family (WebRTC, SCTP, DTLS, STUN) under one roof.\n\n[Firezone's explainer](https://www.firezone.dev/blog/sans-io) pinned down the shape everyone converged on. Four methods:\n\n- `handle_input(packet, now)`: feed in a datagram you received\n- `handle_timeout(now)`: advance the timers\n- `poll_transmit() -> Option<Transmit>`: pull out a datagram it wants to send\n- `poll_timeout() -> Option<Instant>`: ask when it wants to be woken next\n\nThe load-bearing detail is `now`. Time is always an **injected timestamp**, never sampled from a clock inside the machine. That one discipline is what buys portability, testability, and determinism all at once. It is also why the exact same handshake can run under tokio, inside no_std firmware, and *right here in a browser wasm cell*, which is what the rest of this notebook does.\n\nLet me not overclaim: sans-IO is not free, and I will get to the trade-offs at the end. First, the case in numbers; then let me build one.",
   "cargo_toml": null
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_md_test_surface",
   "order": 1,
   "label": "The multiplication that kills you",
   "cell_type": "Markdown",
   "source": "## The test-surface multiplication\n\nPut a socket inside the protocol logic and every test has to go through the socket. That sounds like an inconvenience, but it is really a **multiplication**. The test surface of I/O-entangled protocol code is a product of three axes:\n\n**protocol branches x transports x failure modes.**\n\nCount them for even this toy handshake. Six branches: first send, accept a matching response, ignore a stale one, retransmit on timeout, double the backoff, give up at the budget. Three transports we are claiming portability across: tokio UDP, a no_std firmware loop, browser wasm. Four failure modes worth exercising: loss, duplication, reordering, delay. That is 6 x 3 x 4 = **72 integration scenarios**, each needing a real socket pair, a real timer, and real elapsed time. The retransmit ladder alone burns **31.5 wall-clock seconds** per run at a 500 ms base timeout with six doubling attempts, because with real timers you actually have to wait.\n\nPull the I/O out and the product collapses into a **sum**: six branch tests as pure function calls (microseconds each, no sleeps), plus one thin adapter test per transport. 6 + 3 = 9. The numbers are directional rather than precise, but the shape of the argument is the point: I/O inside the logic *multiplies*; I/O at the edge *adds*.\n\nThere is a second, quieter cost. Socket tests are the classic flaky suite: they race the kernel, they depend on timing, and they fail on loaded CI machines at 2 a.m. A pure state machine cannot flake, because there is nothing nondeterministic left to race.",
   "cargo_toml": null
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_1",
   "order": 2,
   "label": "The whole protocol is an enum and a match",
   "cell_type": "Markdown",
   "source": "## The whole protocol is an enum and a match\n\nA sans-IO handshake is *just* an enum for the state, a `Vec` for the outbound queue, and four methods that pattern-match. No async, no traits, no dependencies. Open the **Shared Source** panel and read the state machine top to bottom: it is about **eighty lines**, and everything below it in the panel is only the rendering glue that draws it for this notebook.\n\nThe toy protocol is a **STUN-style binding request**: a client behind a NAT asks a server what public address it appears to come from, and the server replies with the mapped port. If that reply gets lost, the client retransmits on a timer, backing off each time per [RFC 5389](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5389), and eventually gives up. Four states cover the whole story:\n\n- **Idle**: constructed, nothing sent yet\n- **Waiting**: request in flight, retransmit timer armed\n- **Bound**: the server answered, we learned our mapped port\n- **Failed**: the retransmit budget ran out\n\nEvery transition is driven by one of the four methods, and *not one of them* reaches for a socket or the clock. Watch it happen, first by hand, then in a loop.",
   "cargo_toml": null
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_2",
   "order": 3,
   "label": "Meet the machine",
   "cell_type": "Code",
   "source": "// A transaction on our own clock, in milliseconds. No socket in sight.\nlet mut binding = shared::StunBinding::new(0x1a2b_3c4d, 0, 500, 7);\n\nlet mut trace: Vec<String> = Vec::new();\ntrace.push(format!(\n    \"new()            -> {}\",\n    shared::state_label(binding.state())\n));\n\n// The machine already queued its first request. Ask for it.\nlet first = binding.poll_transmit();\ntrace.push(format!(\"poll_transmit()  -> {first:?}\"));\ntrace.push(format!(\n    \"poll_timeout()   -> wake me at {:?}\",\n    binding.poll_timeout()\n));\n\n// Pretend it hit the wire, reached the server, and a reply came back at t = 120.\nbinding.handle_input(\n    shared::Packet::Response { txid: 0x1a2b_3c4d, mapped_port: 51820 },\n    120,\n);\ntrace.push(format!(\n    \"handle_input()   -> {}\",\n    shared::state_label(binding.state())\n));\ntrace.push(format!(\"poll_timeout()   -> {:?}\", binding.poll_timeout()));\n\nlet listing = format!(\n    \"<pre style=\\\"background:#161625;color:#e0e0ea;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;\\\n     font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;font-size:13px;\\\n     line-height:1.6;margin:0\\\">{}</pre>\",\n    trace.join(\"\\n\"),\n);\n\n(Html(listing), Svg(shared::render_diagram(binding.state())))",
   "cargo_toml": null
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_md_shape",
   "order": 4,
   "label": "Events in, questions out",
   "cell_type": "Markdown",
   "source": "## The shape: events in, questions out\n\nYou just drove the machine by hand: two handlers in, two polls out. The contract, stated precisely:\n\n**Everything crossing the boundary is plain data.** Inputs are values: a datagram plus a timestamp, or a bare timestamp meaning \"time passed\". Outputs are values too: a datagram the machine *wants* sent, or a deadline it *wants* honored. Some crates literally define an `enum Input` and an `enum Output` and funnel everything through two functions; ours splits the input edge into `handle_input` and `handle_timeout` and the output edge into `poll_transmit` and `poll_timeout`. Same shape, different spelling.\n\n**Outputs are polled, never pushed.** The machine never calls you. No callbacks, no channels, no futures. After *any* input you drain `poll_transmit` until it returns `None`, then ask `poll_timeout` for the next deadline; both answers can change after every event, so you re-ask every time. That drain-then-rearm rhythm is the entire caller contract.\n\nThe tempting version this replaces reads beautifully and tests terribly:\n\n```rust\n// Looks clean. Welds the protocol to one runtime, one transport, one clock.\nasync fn bind(socket: &UdpSocket) -> Result<u16> {\n    for attempt in 0..7 {\n        socket.send(&request).await?;\n        match timeout(rto * 2u32.pow(attempt), socket.recv(&mut buf)).await {\n            Ok(n) => return parse_response(&buf[..n?]),\n            Err(_) => continue, // silence: retransmit with doubled rto\n        }\n    }\n    bail!(\"no answer after 7 attempts\")\n}\n```\n\nThe appeal is that it reads top to bottom. The price is that the state (which attempt you are on, the current backoff) lives in a compiler-generated future you cannot inspect, and every branch only executes against a live socket. The sans-IO version stores the same state as **named fields**: `attempt`, `deadline`, an outbox. Nothing is hidden, so nothing needs a socket to observe.\n\nOne consequence of inputs-as-data before we wire up a network: validation is a pattern match, not a parser bolted to a socket. Watch the machine ignore a response meant for a different transaction.",
   "cargo_toml": null
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_stale_txid",
   "order": 5,
   "label": "Wrong transaction id? The machine shrugs",
   "cell_type": "Code",
   "source": "// A response is just a value, so validation is a pattern match. Feed the\n// machine an answer meant for someone else's transaction and watch it shrug.\nlet mut binding = shared::StunBinding::new(0x0bad_f00d, 0, 500, 7);\nlet _ = binding.poll_transmit(); // the request is \"on the wire\"\n\nlet mut trace: Vec<String> = Vec::new();\ntrace.push(format!(\n    \"request sent      -> {}\",\n    shared::state_label(binding.state())\n));\n\n// Wrong transaction id: some other flow's reply. Ignored, state unchanged.\nbinding.handle_input(\n    shared::Packet::Response { txid: 0xffff_ffff, mapped_port: 9 },\n    80,\n);\ntrace.push(format!(\n    \"stale txid @ 80   -> {}\",\n    shared::state_label(binding.state())\n));\n\n// The real reply lands 50 ms later. Matching txid: accepted, state advances.\nbinding.handle_input(\n    shared::Packet::Response { txid: 0x0bad_f00d, mapped_port: 51820 },\n    130,\n);\ntrace.push(format!(\n    \"real txid @ 130   -> {}\",\n    shared::state_label(binding.state())\n));\n\nlet listing = format!(\n    \"<pre style=\\\"background:#161625;color:#e0e0ea;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;\\\n     font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;font-size:13px;\\\n     line-height:1.6;margin:0\\\">{}</pre>\",\n    trace.join(\"\\n\"),\n);\n\nHtml(listing)",
   "cargo_toml": null
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_3",
   "order": 6,
   "label": "You own the event loop",
   "cell_type": "Markdown",
   "source": "## You own the event loop\n\nAdvocates love this part and skeptics grumble about it: the machine does not run itself. **You** own the loop. You pull datagrams out with `poll_transmit`, you decide what happens to them on the wire, you advance the clock, and you hand replies back with `handle_input`. The machine only ever answers one question: *given what you just told me, what now?*\n\nThat is more work, and I will own that. It is also what makes the pattern useful: the loop can be a **synthetic network** with no network in it. The `run` driver in the Shared Source does exactly that:\n\n- a deterministic **LCG** (a four-line pseudo-random generator, no `rand` dependency) flips a weighted coin to drop packets\n- the clock is a plain `u64` of milliseconds that I *jump* straight to the next deadline, so a twelve-second backoff simulates in nanoseconds\n- every step is logged, so the diagrams and tables below are just a rendering of what actually happened\n\nSame seed, same drops, same result, forever. Let me run it three ways: a clean network, a lossy one, and a black hole.",
   "cargo_toml": null
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_4",
   "order": 7,
   "label": "A clean run: zero loss",
   "cell_type": "Code",
   "source": "// A friendly network: zero loss. The machine binds on the very first try.\nlet (final_state, log) = shared::run(0x1a2b_3c4d, 1, 500, 7, 0);\n\n(\n    Svg(shared::render_diagram(final_state)),\n    Html(shared::render_log(&log)),\n)",
   "cargo_toml": null
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_5",
   "order": 8,
   "label": "A lossy run that recovers",
   "cell_type": "Code",
   "source": "// 45% loss on every hop, with a generous retransmit budget. The seed is fixed,\n// so the exact sequence of drops below reproduces byte for byte: that is the trick.\nlet (final_state, log) = shared::run(0x1a2b_3c4d, 12, 500, 7, 45);\n\n(\n    Svg(shared::render_diagram(final_state)),\n    Html(shared::render_log(&log)),\n)",
   "cargo_toml": null
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_6",
   "order": 9,
   "label": "When the network never answers",
   "cell_type": "Code",
   "source": "// A black hole: 100% loss. Every request vanishes, the timers fire, and the\n// machine walks its budget down to a clean Failed. No panic, no leak, no hang.\nlet (final_state, log) = shared::run(0x1a2b_3c4d, 1, 400, 5, 100);\n\n(\n    Svg(shared::render_diagram(final_state)),\n    Html(shared::render_log(&log)),\n)",
   "cargo_toml": null
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_md_timers",
   "order": 10,
   "label": "Timers without clocks",
   "cell_type": "Markdown",
   "source": "## Timers without clocks\n\nTimers are where most protocol code quietly reaches for the wall. A retransmit needs a timeout; a timeout needs a clock; a clock means `Instant::now()`; and now your logic is welded to real time. Sans-IO breaks the weld with one move: **a deadline is data, not a callback.**\n\nThe machine never sets a timer. It computes a deadline from the `now` you handed it, stores that deadline as a plain field, and reports it back when you call `poll_timeout`. Honoring the deadline is your job, and how you honor it depends entirely on where the machine is running: under tokio it becomes `sleep_until`, on firmware it becomes a hardware compare register, in this notebook it becomes a `u64` I simply *assign*. The machine cannot tell the difference, because from where it sits, time is just a number that goes up.\n\nThe caller can lie about time, and lying about time is a feature. The full RFC 5389 ladder for this handshake (500 ms base, doubling, six attempts) spans **31.5 seconds** of protocol time. With real timers, that is 31.5 seconds per test run, times every test that touches a retransmit. With injected time, the next cell walks the entire ladder against a network that never answers and finishes in microseconds: read the deadline, jump the clock to it, call `handle_timeout`, repeat. Watch the gap column double exactly on schedule.",
   "cargo_toml": null
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_backoff_ladder",
   "order": 11,
   "label": "Walking the backoff ladder in microseconds",
   "cell_type": "Code",
   "source": "// Deadlines are data. Walk the full retransmit ladder against total silence,\n// jumping the clock straight to each deadline: 31.5 simulated seconds, ~0 real.\nlet mut binding = shared::StunBinding::new(0xfeed_beef, 0, 500, 6);\nlet mut rows = String::new();\nlet mut clock: u64 = 0;\n\nfor _ in 0..8 {\n    while binding.poll_transmit().is_some() {} // drain; nobody is listening\n    let shared::State::Waiting { attempt, deadline } = binding.state() else {\n        break;\n    };\n    rows.push_str(&format!(\n        \"<tr style=\\\"border-top:1px solid #2a2a44\\\">\\\n         <td style=\\\"padding:6px 12px\\\">{attempt}</td>\\\n         <td style=\\\"padding:6px 12px\\\">{clock} ms</td>\\\n         <td style=\\\"padding:6px 12px\\\">{deadline} ms</td>\\\n         <td style=\\\"padding:6px 12px;color:#ffd166\\\">{} ms</td></tr>\",\n        deadline - clock,\n    ));\n    clock = deadline; // the caller owns time, so the caller can skip it\n    binding.handle_timeout(clock);\n}\n\nlet table = format!(\n    \"<table style=\\\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;background:#161625;\\\n     color:#e0e0ea;font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;\\\n     font-size:13px;border-radius:8px;overflow:hidden\\\">\\\n     <thead><tr style=\\\"background:#20203a;color:#9a9ab5;text-align:left\\\">\\\n     <th style=\\\"padding:6px 12px\\\">attempt</th>\\\n     <th style=\\\"padding:6px 12px\\\">armed at</th>\\\n     <th style=\\\"padding:6px 12px\\\">deadline</th>\\\n     <th style=\\\"padding:6px 12px\\\">gap</th></tr></thead>\\\n     <tbody>{rows}</tbody></table>\\\n     <div style=\\\"color:#9a9ab5;font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;\\\n     font-size:12px;padding:8px 2px\\\">final state: {}</div>\",\n    shared::state_label(binding.state()),\n);\n\nHtml(table)",
   "cargo_toml": null
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_md_determinism",
   "order": 12,
   "label": "Determinism is the payoff",
   "cell_type": "Markdown",
   "source": "## Determinism\n\nAdd up what the machine is *not* allowed to touch (no sockets, no clock, no ambient randomness) and you get the property everything in this notebook has been building toward: **a run is a pure function of its arguments.** The entire world of a run is five numbers: a transaction id, a seed, a base timeout, an attempt budget, a loss rate. Same five numbers, same run, byte for byte, forever.\n\nThat changes what a bug report *is*. Against a real network, a protocol bug arrives as a packet capture, a timestamp, and a prayer that you can recreate the conditions. Against a sans-IO machine, a bug arrives as a **seed**. Whatever packet ordering wedged the state machine, the seed replays it on your laptop, in a debugger, as many times as it takes.\n\nIt also changes what a fuzzer can promise. Fuzzing I/O-entangled code means fighting nondeterminism the whole way down, and half the crashes will not reproduce. Fuzzing a pure machine means every crash arrives with its reproducer attached, because the input *is* the reproducer. The two cells below make both claims concrete: first the same lossy run twice with the logs diffed entry by entry, then a 200-seed sweep run twice, fingerprinted, and asserted identical.",
   "cargo_toml": null
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_7",
   "order": 13,
   "label": "Determinism means replay",
   "cell_type": "Code",
   "source": "// Determinism is testable, not a slogan. Run the same lossy scenario twice and\n// diff the logs. A protocol that read the clock or the socket could never\n// promise this.\nlet (state_a, log_a) = shared::run(0x1a2b_3c4d, 12, 500, 7, 45);\nlet (state_b, log_b) = shared::run(0x1a2b_3c4d, 12, 500, 7, 45);\n\nlet identical = state_a == state_b\n    && log_a.len() == log_b.len()\n    && log_a.iter().zip(&log_b).all(|(x, y)| {\n        x.clock == y.clock && x.kind == y.kind && x.detail == y.detail && x.state == y.state\n    });\n\nlet verdict = format!(\n    \"<div style=\\\"background:#161625;color:#e0e0ea;padding:18px;border-radius:8px;\\\n     font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;font-size:14px;line-height:1.8\\\">\\\n     two independent runs, one seed<br>\\\n     steps recorded: <b style=\\\"color:#4fc3f7\\\">{}</b> and <b style=\\\"color:#4fc3f7\\\">{}</b><br>\\\n     final state: <b style=\\\"color:#bb9cf0\\\">{}</b> and <b style=\\\"color:#bb9cf0\\\">{}</b><br>\\\n     logs identical: <b style=\\\"color:{}\\\">{}</b></div>\",\n    log_a.len(),\n    log_b.len(),\n    shared::state_label(state_a),\n    shared::state_label(state_b),\n    if identical { \"#3ecf8e\" } else { \"#ff6b6b\" },\n    identical,\n);\n\nHtml(verdict)",
   "cargo_toml": null
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_fuzz_sweep",
   "order": 14,
   "label": "A 200-seed sweep, twice",
   "cell_type": "Code",
   "source": "// A poor man's fuzzer: 200 seeds, each a different pattern of packet loss.\n// Run the whole sweep twice and demand identical results, run by run.\nlet sweep = || {\n    let mut bound = 0u32;\n    let mut failed = 0u32;\n    let mut fingerprint = 0u64;\n    for seed in 0..200u64 {\n        let (state, log) = shared::run(0x1a2b_3c4d, seed, 500, 7, 45);\n        match state {\n            shared::State::Bound { .. } => bound += 1,\n            shared::State::Failed => failed += 1,\n            _ => {}\n        }\n        // Fold every log length into one number; any divergence anywhere shifts it.\n        fingerprint = fingerprint.wrapping_mul(1_000_003).wrapping_add(log.len() as u64);\n    }\n    (bound, failed, fingerprint)\n};\n\nlet (bound_a, failed_a, fp_a) = sweep();\nlet (bound_b, failed_b, fp_b) = sweep();\n\n// If any of the 400 runs diverged, this cell traps instead of rendering.\nassert_eq!((bound_a, failed_a, fp_a), (bound_b, failed_b, fp_b));\n\nHtml(format!(\n    \"<div style=\\\"background:#161625;color:#e0e0ea;padding:18px;border-radius:8px;\\\n     font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;font-size:14px;line-height:1.8\\\">\\\n     200 seeds at 45% loss, swept twice<br>\\\n     bound: <b style=\\\"color:#3ecf8e\\\">{bound_a}</b> / failed: \\\n     <b style=\\\"color:#ff6b6b\\\">{failed_a}</b> (sweep two: {bound_b} / {failed_b})<br>\\\n     sweep fingerprints: <b style=\\\"color:#4fc3f7\\\">{fp_a:#018x}</b> vs \\\n     <b style=\\\"color:#4fc3f7\\\">{fp_b:#018x}</b><br>\\\n     identical: <b style=\\\"color:#3ecf8e\\\">{}</b></div>\",\n    fp_a == fp_b,\n))",
   "cargo_toml": null
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_8",
   "order": 15,
   "label": "The same eighty lines, everywhere",
   "cell_type": "Markdown",
   "source": "## The same eighty lines, everywhere\n\nNothing in the Shared Source knows it is running in a browser. That is the entire return on the discipline, and it shows up in three concrete places.\n\n**It is portable.** The handshake has no runtime and no I/O, so it drops into a tokio task, a no_std embedded loop, or a wasm cell without changing a line. You just saw the third case: that state machine compiled to `wasm32-unknown-unknown` and ran in a Web Worker to draw these diagrams. It never noticed.\n\n**It is testable without a network.** Scroll back to the lossy run. I simulated 45% packet loss, watched the retransmit timers fire, and confirmed recovery, with *zero* sockets and no mocking framework anywhere. The \"network\" was a coin flip. Testing the failure path of a real socket stack is a genuine pain; here it is a function argument.\n\n**It is deterministic, which means bugs replay.** The determinism cell ran the same seed twice and diffed the logs byte for byte. When a fuzzer finds the one packet sequence that wedges your protocol, it hands you a seed, and that seed reproduces the exact failure on your laptop every single time. A protocol that reads the clock or the socket directly can never promise that.\n\nNow the costs, because sans-IO is not a free lunch:\n\n- **You write the event loop, and the glue is real.** Every consumer re-implements the poll-transmit, arm-the-timer, feed-the-input dance. It is boilerplate, and it is easy to get subtly wrong.\n- **The ergonomics can feel inside-out.** An `async fn` reads top to bottom; a hand-cranked state machine does not. For a simple request and response, plenty of people call this overkill, and the discourse about *when* it earns its keep is very much alive.\n- **You give up the free state that `.await` keeps for you.** Everything the machine must remember becomes an explicit field. That is exactly why it is inspectable, and also exactly why there is more to type.\n\nMy take: for anything with retransmits, congestion control, or a spec you have to match byte for byte, the determinism and testability win by a mile. For a one-shot request, open a socket and go home.",
   "cargo_toml": null
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_md_production",
   "order": 16,
   "label": "How the production crates structure it",
   "cell_type": "Markdown",
   "source": "## How the production crates structure it\n\nThe toy in this notebook is small, but the layering is a faithful miniature of how the real crates are cut.\n\n**[quinn-proto](https://docs.rs/quinn-proto)** is the clearest example, because the split is visible right in the crate graph. `quinn-proto` holds the QUIC state machines (`Endpoint` and `Connection`) and contains no sockets and no async: events and datagrams go in via `handle_event` and `handle_timeout`, and outbound work comes back out via `poll_transmit` and `poll_timeout`. Recognize those names? The [quinn](https://docs.rs/quinn) crate is the tokio adapter that owns the sockets and timers, and `quinn-udp` is the platform socket layer underneath. Three crates, one protocol, and the entire QUIC spec (streams, congestion control, connection migration, all of it) lives in the pure layer. If the pattern scales to QUIC, it scales to your protocol.\n\n**[h2](https://github.com/python-hyper/h2)** is where the *name* came from. Cory Benfield's Python HTTP/2 library, circa 2016, was built as a protocol object you feed raw bytes: parsed events come out one side, `data_to_send()` hands you wire bytes on the other. The [sans-io documentation site](https://sans-io.readthedocs.io) that grew out of that work is still the best statement of the philosophy, and the Rust ecosystem borrowed the vocabulary wholesale.\n\n**[str0m](https://github.com/algesten/str0m)** pushes it furthest: an entire WebRTC stack, fully synchronous, every public API taking an explicit `Instant`.\n\nDifferent protocols, different languages, one converged contract: data in with an injected clock, outputs polled, nothing async in the core.",
   "cargo_toml": null
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_md_overkill",
   "order": 17,
   "label": "When sans-IO is overkill",
   "cell_type": "Markdown",
   "source": "## When sans-IO is overkill\n\nHonesty section: I would not reach for this pattern everywhere, and the failure mode of pattern enthusiasm is applying it to code that never needed it.\n\nSkip sans-IO when all three of these hold:\n\n- **One transport, forever.** An internal service speaking HTTP to one other service is not going to grow a no_std port. The portability you would be buying has no buyer.\n- **No timers or retransmits of your own.** If TCP and TLS below you already handle reliability, your \"protocol\" has no interesting time-dependent state to extract. A request/response with no retries is a function call; wrapping it in a state machine adds ceremony, not testability.\n- **No conformance burden.** Nobody will ever fuzz your internal JSON endpoint against a spec, and no seed will ever be attached to one of its bug reports.\n\nIn that world the sans-IO tax buys nothing, and the tax is real: every consumer writes the drive loop (drain `poll_transmit`, re-arm the timer, feed inputs, repeat), which lands around 50 to 100 lines of glue per integration, and the classic mistake (forgetting to drain the outbox after `handle_timeout`) produces a protocol that mysteriously stalls. Write that loop once, for one transport, and you paid for generality you will never spend.\n\nThe flip-side triggers are just as crisp: a second transport on the roadmap, retransmit or congestion logic of your own, a spec you must match byte for byte, or a bug class that only replay will catch. Any one of those and the pattern starts paying rent immediately. For a mental model: sans-IO earns its keep when the *protocol*, not the transport underneath it, is where your bugs live.",
   "cargo_toml": null
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_9",
   "order": 18,
   "label": "Who is carrying this",
   "cell_type": "Markdown",
   "source": "## Who is carrying this\n\nSans-IO is how a growing slice of the Rust networking ecosystem is actually built, and the roster is worth knowing.\n\n- **[str0m](https://github.com/algesten/str0m)** (Martin Algesten): a fully synchronous, sans-IO WebRTC stack, and since January 2026 the new home of **[sctp-proto](https://github.com/algesten/sctp-proto)** as well.\n- **[quinn-proto](https://docs.rs/quinn-proto)**: the QUIC state machine underneath Quinn, and the pattern's most-cited proof that it scales all the way up to a real, complicated protocol.\n- **[Firezone](https://www.firezone.dev/blog/sans-io)**: the team whose explainer handed everyone the shared vocabulary, four methods and an injected clock, that this notebook borrows wholesale.\n\nThe pattern is *old* (it is just a state machine, the thing we have been teaching in school forever) and *newly fashionable*, because Rust's `async` story made the cost of **not** separating I/O from logic impossible to ignore. You just watched it run: a protocol that never touches a socket will run anywhere you can call four functions, a notebook cell included.\n\nGo open the Shared Source and change the loss rate. The state machine underneath is about eighty lines, and every run replays exactly. Break it, replay it, fix it.",
   "cargo_toml": null
  }
 ]
}